Runtime backend creation test (backend/)
=======================================

The backend test checks OTel filter instances inherited by 'add backend' from
named defaults.  It runs HAProxy with and without 'insecure-fork-wanted', checks
that the startup backend exists, and asks the admin CLI to create a backend at
runtime.  The test checks the CLI result and the registered backend names, and
requires the OTel parser's diagnostic when runtime creation is rejected.

The runner starts its own HAProxy instances and stops them with USR1, using only
its own process IDs.  Each instance has a private Unix socket in a temporary
directory.  It sends no traffic and needs neither an origin server nor a local
collector.  The SDK exporters write to /dev/null; the trace batch processor and
metric reader use background threads.


Requirements
------------

Use Python 3 and a HAProxy build with the OTel filter class and the runtime 'add
backend' command.  The filter class exists from HAProxy 3.5 on; 'add backend' is
older.  The rejection exists only in a build whose libc provides RLIMIT_NPROC,
which HAProxy clears for the worker; a build without it fails the rejection
check instead of reporting a passing test.

The fixture sets 'chroot /' so runtime creation can still read the OTel and YAML
files.  The runner supplies absolute configuration paths through environment
variables, since HAProxy changes its working directory during startup.  This is
an isolated test configuration, not a suggested production security setting.


Files
-----

  backend/haproxy.cfg          Named defaults, the filter instances and the
                               group action
  backend/haproxy-insecure.cfg Enables 'insecure-fork-wanted' for the allow run
  backend/otel.cfg             One OTel configuration section per instance,
                               the group definition included
  backend/otel.yml             SDK providers and local exporters
  run-backend.py               Runner and assertions against the live admin CLI


Running the test
----------------

From the test/ directory, give the runner the HAProxy executable to use.  The
binary path is required, so invoking the runner without arguments prints help
and exits without starting an instance:

  % ./run-backend.py ../../haproxy/haproxy
  % ./run-backend.py --help

The runner prints a PASS for each accepted startup and each expected runtime
creation result.  It exits 0 on success, EX_SOFTWARE (70) when a check fails or
the executable cannot be started, EX_NOINPUT (66) when the executable file is
missing, and EX_UNAVAILABLE (69) when it is not executable.  Invalid command
arguments return EX_USAGE (64); explicit help exits 0.

If a check fails and stopping HAProxy also fails, the runner reports the cleanup
failure separately and keeps the original check failure as the final diagnostic.

The final output line names a new directory under _logs/.  Its deny.log and
allow.log files hold HAProxy's output; deny-cli.log and allow-cli.log hold the
commands and their replies.  These logs remain after the runner removes its
private socket directory and stops its instances.


What is checked
---------------

The fixture declares OTel filter instances in its named defaults section, one
enabled and one written without the 'enabled' word, and its 'backend startup'
inherits them during startup.

The section also carries an 'otel-group' action, which the proxies inheriting
the section share, so the resolution of that action runs for the backend created
at runtime as well: an action that cannot be resolved there fails the creation.
The runner then issues these commands on the instance's admin socket:

  show filter classes request
  show filter instances startup request
  show filter instance startup otel/backend-test
  show filter instance startup otel/backend-test-off
  show backend
  add backend runtime from otel-backend-defaults
  show backend

The class list of the request side must name the 'otel' class, the instance list
must name the instances in the order they were declared, and every instance
report must carry the state 'enabled' and 'disabled'.  An instance inherited
from the defaults section reads as 'enabled, inherited' or 'disabled, inherited'
there, which the runner accepts.

Without 'insecure-fork-wanted', the startup backend must exist, but runtime
creation must fail and leave no registered 'runtime' backend.  The HAProxy log
must carry the OTel parser's diagnostic, not just an unrelated creation error:

  runtime creation requires 'insecure-fork-wanted'

With 'insecure-fork-wanted', the startup backend must still exist, and runtime
creation must succeed with 'runtime' present in the backend list.  This checks
the startup/runtime distinction in flt_otel_parse_instance(), where the option
is required only after HAProxy clears MODE_STARTING.

The test ends at backend creation.  It does not publish the backend, register a
server or send requests through it; traffic and telemetry belong to the other
scenario tests.
